brief_summary:
After forefoot surgery, patients often have moderate to severe pain. To control it better, anesthesiologists use, among other things, "nerve blocks": an anesthetic is injected near the nerves to numb only the foot area. This can be done either behind the knee (popliteal sciatic block) or at the ankle (distal ankle blocks). These blocks are part of a pain-relief approach that combines several methods. However, there are few comparisons of these techniques as they are actually used in everyday hospital practice.

The goal is to compare, after surgery, the level of pain and the amount of painkillers patients need depending on three approaches:

1. No nerve block (patients receive only the usual painkillers);
2. A block behind the knee (popliteal sciatic block) given as a single injection ("single-shot"), using ultrasound to see the nerves and guide the needle precisely;
3. A block at the ankle, performed without ultrasound, relying only on body landmarks ("anatomical landmark technique"), meaning the doctor locates the nerves by seeing and feeling the anatomy.

In short: the study aims to determine which method best relieves pain and reduces the need for painkilling drugs after forefoot surgery.

A Phase I, Randomized, Double-blind, Placebo-controlled, Single and Multiple Ascending Dose escalation, and Food-Effect Study of the Safety, Tolerability, Pharmacokinetics and Pharmacodynamics of INT-1868 Tablets in Healthy Adult Participants.

* Primary Objectives:

  1\. To evaluate the safety and tolerability of INT-1868 tablets after single and multiple doses in healthy adult participants;
* Secondary Objectives:

  1\. To evaluate the PK characteristics of INT-1868 tablets after single and multiple doses in healthy adult participants; 2. To evaluate the effect of food on the PK characteristics of INT-1868 tablets;
* Other Objectives:

  1. To evaluate the PD characteristics of INT-1868 tablets after single and multiple doses in healthy adult participants;
  2. To evaluate the PK characteristics of INT-1868 tablet metabolites (if applicable) after single and multiple doses in healthy adult participants;
  3. To assess the effect of INT-1868 tablets on C-QTc;
  4. To preliminarily evaluate the metabolic characteristics of INT-1868 tablets in humans.
